{"id":94,"text":"In a town at the foot of a mountain, a lantern seller named Omar had a shop that was always busy during the long winter nights. His lanterns were sturdy and bright, and travellers praised them.\n\nOne evening, a strange customer entered. He was a blind man, led by a dog. “I’d like to buy your best lantern,” he said.\n\nOmar was puzzled. “Forgive me, sir, but why would a blind man need a lantern?”\n\nThe man smiled. “Because of other people. When I walk in the dark with a lantern, others can see me and avoid walking into me. My light is not for my own eyes. It’s a gift to everyone on the road.”\n\nOmar was deeply moved. He gave the man the best lantern in the shop and refused to take any money. Afterwards, he often thought about the visitor. He had always seen his work as selling light to those who needed it, but the blind man had shown him a deeper truth: light has value when it is shared.\n\nFrom then on, Omar left a lantern burning outside his shop every night, so that any lonely traveller could find their way, even without making a purchase. Strangely, his business doubled.\n\nMoral: The light we carry helps not only ourselves but everyone who walks near us.","tr":null,"gl":{"in":"ichida, -da","a":"bir (noaniq artikl)","town":"shaharcha","at":"-da, -ga","the":"(aniq artikl)","foot":"etak (tog' etagi); oyoq","of":"-ning; -dan","mountain":"tog'","lantern":"fonar","seller":"sotuvchi","named":"ismli","omar":"Umar (ism)","had":"bor edi; -gan edi","shop":"do'kon","that":"u, o'sha; -ki","was":"edi","always":"doim, har doim","busy":"band","during":"davomida","long":"uzoq, uzun","winter":"qish","nights":"tunlar","his":"uning","lanterns":"fonarlar","were":"edi(lar)","sturdy":"mustahkam","and":"va","bright":"yorqin","travellers":"sayyohlar","praised":"maqtadi","them":"ularni, ularga","one":"bir, bitta","evening":"kechqurun","strange":"g'alati","customer":"xaridor","entered":"kirdi","he":"u (erkak)","blind":"ko'r","man":"odam, kishi","led":"olib bordi, boshq